</div> 
 <p></p>
 <p></p>
 <p >
 	<span class="fontStyle">This letter is to advise you of your facility's compliance with New York State regulations requiring expedited HIV testing in the labor and delivery setting when a pregnant person's HIV status is unknown to prevent perinatal HIV transmission. Additionally, facilities should ensure compliance with New York State public health laws requiring third trimester syphilis screening and at least one screening for hepatitis C during every pregnancy to <a href="https://www.health.ny.gov/community/health_equity/ending_preventable_epidemics.htm">end preventable epidemics</a>. </span>
 </p>
 
<p></p>
 
<p class="mT15">
<span class="boldUnderLineFontStyle">Prenatal HIV, Hepatitis C, and Syphilis Testing</span>:<br/>
<span>
<span class="fontStyle">The statewide prenatal HIV testing rate for the ${QUARTER_STRING!""} was ${PRENATAL_PERCENTAGE!""}%. Providers should be commended for this sustained success. Building on this success, New York State recommends that the <a href="https://www.health.ny.gov/diseases/communicable/hepatitis/hepatitis_c/testing/providers.htm">required prenatal hepatitis C antibody screening</a> be performed at the first prenatal visit when HIV, syphilis, and other obstetrical lab work is obtained. New York State also strongly recommends that the <a href="https://www.health.ny.gov/diseases/communicable/congenital_syphilis/providers/docs/third_trimester_screening_letter.pdf">required third trimester syphilis screening</a> be coupled with the <a href="https://www.hivguidelines.org/guideline/hiv-testing-pregnancy/?mytab=tab_3&amp;mycollection=hiv-perinatal">recommended repeat HIV test</a> between 28- and 32- weeks' gestation. The statewide rate for testing pregnant individuals for HIV a second time before delivery during these quarters was ${SEC_PRENATAL_PERCENTAGE!""}%. Repeat HIV testing is necessary to identify infection acquired in later pregnancy and postpartum, especially for people who are breast/chestfeeding. To identify seroconversion and prevent perinatal HIV transmission, facilities should review their data and implement a quality approach to increasing the number/rate of the recommended repeat prenatal test before delivery. Immediate HIV testing is also recommended for a pregnant or breast/chestfeeding person who presents with a viral syndrome consistent with <a href="https://www.hivguidelines.org/guideline/hiv-acute/?mycollection=hiv-testing-acute-infection#tab_1">acute HIV infection</a> (e.g., influenza/mononucleosis, COVID-19-like illnesses), even if the person tested HIV negative earlier in pregnancy.  
</span>
</span>
</p>
<p></p>


<p class="mT15">
<span class="boldUnderLineFontStyle">Clinical Guidelines</span>:<br/>
<span>
<span class="fontStyle">Significant revisions and updates were made to the national <a href="https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/guidelines/perinatal/whats-new">Perinatal HIV Clinical Guidelines</a> including</span> <span class="italicFont" >Antiretroviral Management of Infants With In Utero, Intrapartum or Breastfeeding Exposure or HIV Infection.</span> <span class="fontStyle">New York State clinicians may call the <a href="https://ceitraining.org/">Clinical Education Initiative</a> (CEI) hotline 1-866-631-2342, or the <a href="https://nccc.ucsf.edu/clinician-consultation/perinatal-hiv-aids/">National Perinatal HIV/AIDS Clinical Consultation Center</a> hotline 1-844-275-6222, with questions.  
</span>
</span>
</p>
<p></p>

<p class="mT15">
<span class="boldUnderLineFontStyle">Health Equity</span>:<br/>
<span>
<span class="fontStyle">Among perinatal populations, it is critical to identify and address <a href="https://www.health.ny.gov/community/health_equity/">social drivers of health</a> (e.g., transportation, housing) and <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/nchhstp/priorities/hiv-prevention.html">syndemics</a> (e.g., HIV/sexually transmitted infections, hepatitis C, substance use, mental health conditions) to reduce maternal-fetal/child morbidity and mortality. In addition to reviewing policies and procedures for HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C screening during pregnancy, labor/delivery, postpartum, and/or newborn periods to improve early diagnosis and treatment and to reduce the risk of vertical transmission, policies should include resources to address <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/about/priorities/why-is-addressing-sdoh-important.html">social drivers of health</a> including screening tools and active referral, linkage and navigation to care management and supportive services. 
</span>
</span>
</p>
<p></p>

<p class="mT15">
<span class="boldUnderLineFontStyle">Performance on Maternal [Birth Parent]/Newborn Expedited HIV Testing</span>:<br/>
<span class="fontStyle">The New York State Department of Health monitors compliance with expedited HIV testing regulations through the data collected by your staff on the <a href="https://www.wadsworth.org/programs/newborn/screening/providers/forms">Newborn Screening Blood Collection Form (DOH-1514)</a>. The data have been compiled on your facility's performance for the ${DATE_RANGE!""}. Each quarter is presented in Attachment A</span>. <span class="fontStyle">Information specific to any case(s) during each quarter </span><span class="italicFont">only</span><span class="fontStyle"> where expedited HIV testing was indicated but was either not done or not documented ("missed opportunity") is provided in </span><span class="boldFontStyle">${ATTACHMENT_B_QUARTER}</span>. <span class="fontStyle">Any case of missing or discordant HIV information reported should be reviewed to determine why documentation of prenatal or expedited testing was omitted. A continuous quality improvement process should be implemented to determine the cause(s) for missed opportunities and to correct any issue(s) identified to ensure pregnant people receive New York State recommended standard of care to avert perinatal HIV transmission.</span>
</p>
<p></p>
<p class="mT15">
<span class="italicFont" >This information is being provided for continuous quality improvement activities only; it is not necessary for your staff to notify us of the results of your continuous quality improvement activities. However, it is expected that all birth facilities will continue to use internal quality improvement strategies to achieve and sustain full compliance.
</span>
</p>

<p></p>

<p class="mT25">
<span class="fontStyle">Thank you for your commitment and ongoing efforts to sustain the elimination of perinatal HIV transmission and to achieve elimination of congenital syphilis and hepatitis C. If you have questions about this correspondence or if your staff would like technical assistance, please contact the Perinatal Program at 518-486-6806 or <a href="phpp@health.ny.gov">phpp@health.ny.gov</a>.</span>
</p>
<p></p>
<p class="mT15">
<span class="fontStyle">Sincerely,</span>
</p>
</div>
